@* Toolbar's What doesn't fit page, moved from its Overview: what moves into the … menu first, icons
   before the menu, and wrapping instead. *@

<Heading Level="2">What moves first</Heading>
<TextBlock>When a toolbar runs out of room, its items move into the … menu one at a time. To keep an item in the bar longer, give it a higher <Code>Priority</Code>; items default to 0. To fix where an item goes, set its <Code>Overflow</Code>: <Code>Never</Code> keeps it in the bar, such as the main action, and <Code>Always</Code> puts it only in the menu.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-priority" />
<TextBlock>Items move in this order:</TextBlock>
<List Gap="2" Ordered>
    <ListItem>Items with a lower <Code>Priority</Code> move before items with a higher one.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>Among items with the same priority, the items at the start move first, beginning with the one nearest the middle.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>Then the items at the end move, again beginning with the one nearest the middle. The main action at the far end moves last.</ListItem>
</List>
<TextBlock>A <Code>ToolbarSeparator</Code> leaves the bar when nothing is left on one side of it.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Icons before the menu</Heading>
<TextBlock>A toolbar can show its buttons as icons only before it moves anything into the … menu. Set <Code>CollapseLabels</Code>, and buttons and menus with a <Code>StartIcon</Code> all drop their text at once. The text stays for screen readers and as the tooltip.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-collapse-labels" />
<TextBlock>Narrow the window to see it. Once the icons alone don't fit, items move into the menu as before. An item with <Code>Overflow="ToolbarOverflow.Never"</Code> keeps its text.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Wrapping instead</Heading>
<TextBlock>A toolbar that wraps shows every item, on as many rows as it needs, with no … menu. It suits a toolbar where every control must stay visible. Set <Code>Wrap</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-wrap" />
